  "question": {
    "text": "Two light waves having the same wavelength <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><mi>&#955;</mi></math> in vacuum are in phase initially. Then the first wave travels a path <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><msub><mi>L</mi><mn>1</mn></msub></math> through a medium of refractive index <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><msub><mi>n</mi><mn>1</mn></msub></math>while the second wave travels a path of length&nbsp;<math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><msub><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">L</mi><mn>2</mn></msub></math> through a medium of refractive index <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><msub><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">n</mi><mn>2</mn></msub></math>. After this the phase difference between the two waves is:"
  },
  "options": [
    {
      "text": "<math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><mfrac><mrow><mn>2</mn><mi>&#960;</mi></mrow><mi>&#955;</mi></mfrac><mfenced><mrow><mfrac><msub><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">L</mi><mn>2</mn></msub><msub><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">n</mi><mn>1</mn></msub></mfrac><mo>-</mo><mfrac><msub><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">L</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><msub><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">n</mi><mn>2</mn></msub></mfrac></mrow></mfenced></math>",
